<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<timers>
    <!-- The one shot re-parse that makes the first run defaults visible on a
         fresh install. Home.xml arms it by writing Skin.String(pov_reload) as
         'armed', and only ever does that inside the guarded first run block, so
         a box that is already stamped never sees this timer start.

         WHY A RELOAD IS NEEDED AT ALL. Kodi resolves <include condition=...> once,
         while it is parsing the XML, and never re-evaluates it; <visible> is the
         tag that is checked continuously. Two of the seeded defaults are read
         only by include conditions: circle_rating (Includes.xml:104 and :1831,
         plus eight view files) and hide_mediaflags (Home.xml:1228,
         DialogSeekBar.xml:54, MyVideoNav.xml:94, MyMusicNav.xml:36,
         MyPlaylist.xml:37, MyPVRRecordings.xml:80). On a profile that has never
         run this skin, every window is parsed BEFORE Home's onload writes those
         two, so the rating control is never built and the media flags are built
         when they should not be. Re-parsing once, immediately after the seed, is
         what puts both right on the first screen the owner sees. Visiting skin
         settings used to be the only thing that forced that re-parse.

         WHY A TIMER AND NOT ReloadSkin() STRAIGHT FROM THE ONLOAD. Two separate
         reasons, both measured in the Kodi 22 source:

           Load actions are dispatched with SendMessage, not SendThreadMessage.
           CGUIAction::ExecuteActions (guilib/GUIAction.cpp:72-81) only posts a
           thread message when EnableSendThreadMessageMode has been called, and
           the only callers are control onfocus/onunfocus
           (GUIControlFactory.cpp:1042) and skin timers
           (SkinTimerManager.cpp:97 and :114). GUIWindow.cpp never calls it for
           m_loadActions, so an onload builtin runs synchronously inside
           CGUIWindow::OnInitWindow. ReloadSkin there tears down the control tree
           that the live stack frame is standing in, and the IsNested() deferral
           in ApplicationSkinHandling.cpp:447 does not catch it because m_iNested
           only counts nested RENDER loops (GUIWindowManager.cpp:1551).

           It would also do nothing on the boot it is meant to fix. On a cold
           start Home is loaded from inside CApplication::Initialize, so
           m_bInitializing is still true and ReloadSkin returns at
           ApplicationSkinHandling.cpp:441 without reloading. Skin timers are
           evaluated from CApplication::Process instead, which cannot run until
           Initialize has returned.

         WHY IT CANNOT LOOP, which matters more than any of the above because a
         reload loop is an unusable box. The onstop actions run in order, and the
         first one rewrites pov_reload to 'done'. The start condition tests for
         exactly 'armed', so the reloaded skin reads 'done' and the timer never
         starts again. The write also reaches disk: UnloadSkin calls
         SaveSettings before Unload (ApplicationSkinHandling.cpp:245), so the new
         value survives the reload rather than waiting on the 500 ms debounce in
         Skin.cpp. Nothing anywhere writes 'armed' except the guarded first run
         block in Home.xml, which by then has already stamped itself. -->
    <timer>
        <name>povfirstrunreload</name>
        <description>One shot skin reload that lets the first run defaults reach the parse time include conditions</description>
        <start reset="true">String.IsEqual(Skin.String(pov_reload),armed)</start>
        <stop>Integer.IsGreaterOrEqual(Skin.TimerElapsedSecs(povfirstrunreload),1)</stop>
        <onstop>Skin.SetString(pov_reload,done)</onstop>
        <onstop>ReloadSkin()</onstop>
    </timer>
    <timer>
        <name>autoclosevideoosd</name>
        <description>Timer to auto close the video OSD (if enabled in the skin settings)</description>
        <start reset="true">Window.IsActive(videoosd) + Skin.HasSetting(OSDAutoClose) + !String.IsEqual(window(home).Property(settingsdialog_content),osd) + !Window.IsActive(osdsubtitlesettings) + !Window.IsActive(osdaudiosettings) + !Window.IsActive(osdvideosettings) + !Window.IsActive(OSDCMSSettings)</start>
        <reset>Window.IsActive(videoosd) + !System.IdleTime(1) + Integer.IsGreaterOrEqual(Skin.TimerElapsedSecs(autoclosevideoosd), 1) | String.IsEqual(window(home).Property(settingsdialog_content),osd) | Window.IsActive(osdsubtitlesettings) | Window.IsActive(osdaudiosettings) | Window.IsActive(osdvideosettings) | Window.IsActive(OSDCMSSettings)</reset>
        <stop>!Window.IsActive(videoosd) | String.IsEmpty(Skin.String(OSDAutoCloseTime)) + Integer.IsGreaterOrEqual(Skin.TimerElapsedSecs(autoclosevideoosd), 4) | !String.IsEmpty(Skin.String(OSDAutoCloseTime)) + Integer.IsGreaterOrEqual(Skin.TimerElapsedSecs(autoclosevideoosd),Skin.Numeric(OSDAutoCloseTime))</stop>
        <onstop>Dialog.Close(videoosd)</onstop>
    </timer>
    <timer>
        <name>1109_topbaroverlay</name>
        <description>A timer that is activated when the topbaroverlay is loaded and stops automatically after 5 seconds (or playback is resumed)</description>
        <start reset="true">[Window.IsActive(videoosd) | Window.IsActive(musicosd) | Window.IsActive(playerprocessinfo)] | [Window.IsActive(1109) + [Player.Seeking | Player.Forwarding | Player.Rewinding | Player.HasPerformedSeek(1)]]</start>
        <reset>Player.Seeking | Player.Forwarding | Player.Rewinding | Player.HasPerformedSeek(1)</reset>
        <stop>Integer.IsGreaterOrEqual(Skin.TimerElapsedSecs(1109_topbaroverlay),5) | Player.Playing</stop>
    </timer>
</timers>
